{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YPvJY9KFewwyjCaauMRX9q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Aa4LbC8tVN8tPubtpYmsUq","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Generation Depth Fingerprinting via Differential Depth Response in Non-Paraphrastic LLM Chains\"\n\nThe paper formulates \"generation depth classification\" (gen-0 / gen-1 / gen-2+) as a new task beyond binary AI-text detection. It introduces Differential Depth Response (DDR): when text is recursively generated through non-paraphrastic chains, central-tendency features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) of NLL distributions drift monotonically with depth, while shape features (entropy max, tail decay) remain near-stationary. This is consistent with a location shift in the NLL distribution.\n\n**Method**: \n- Constructs recursive generation chains (depth 0–4) using base-model LLMs\n- 3 prompt types: continuation, elaboration, inspired rewriting\n- 4 domains: Wikipedia, news, academic, code\n- Extracts 7 features from token-level NLL under reference model (Pythia-410M): 2 surface (TTR, hapax_ratio) + 5 reference (entropy mean/std/min/max, tail_decay)\n- XGBoost classifier with LOGO (leave-one-generator-out) evaluation\n- Primary metric: leak-free binary F1 (gen-1 vs gen-2+, excluding gen-0)\n\n**Key experimental results**:\n- Feature ablation (Table 1): Full 7-feat = 0.820 macro F1, surface-only = 0.763, reference-only = 0.719\n- DDR characterization (Table 2): Drifting features |ρ| > 0.60, stationary |ρ| < 0.12\n- Cross-generator LOGO (Table 3, 5 generators): avg macro F1 = 0.804, binary F1 = 0.694\n- Baseline comparison (Table 4): 7-feat (0.798) > Avg-LogProb (0.765) > LD-Score (0.721) > FastDetectGPT (0.627) > Binoculars (0.579)\n- Cross-model chains (Table 5): binary F1 = 0.830 (+13.6 pp vs single-model)\n- Cross-reference validation (Table 6): 3 reference models all preserve DDR (<1.5 pp range)\n- DDR causality (Table 7): 6/7 Granger-significant, 5.3× permutation importance gap\n- Downstream utility (Table 8): Negative finding — depth curation ≈ random subsampling (TOST p=1.1×10⁻⁴ at ±2%)\n- Instruct degradation (Table 9): Qwen base 0.821 → instruct 0.658 binary F1, feature direction reversal\n- Multi-domain (Table 10): All domains 0.739–0.815 binary F1\n- Large-scale 8B (Table 11): 6-gen LOGO F1 = 0.789, Qwen3-8B held-out = 0.683\n\n**Appendix experiments** (Tables 12–24):\n- Multi-detector ensemble: +2.1 pp 3-class at 2.4× cost (Table 12)\n- 5-class ordinal: ceiling at ~0.50, ordinal methods don't help (Table 13)\n- Temperature sensitivity: stable at t∈[0.5,0.7], degrades at t=0.9 (Table 14)\n- Human edit robustness: graceful degradation, 5% noise = -4.6 pp (Table 15)\n- LD-Score augmentation: +0.5 pp marginal (Table 16)\n- QLoRA end-to-end: 3-class 0.859–0.907, +10–15 pp but 15× cost (Table 17)\n- Length confound: controlled, 0.724 conservative estimate (Table 18)\n- 9-gen evaluation: 0.751, Gemma anomaly from tokenizer mismatch (Table 19)\n- Prompt type comparison: continuation strongest, elaboration weakest (Table 20)\n- Paraphrastic vs non-paraphrastic: 0.62 vs 0.781 (Table 21)\n- TOST stationarity: 3/4 confirmed stationary, entropy_std intermediate (Table 22)\n- Independent seed validation: 0.680 vs 0.694, no leakage (Table 23)\n- Topic drift control: semantic similarity increases with depth but DDR independent (Table 24)\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are the evaluation protocols (LOGO, LOFO) appropriate? Any methodological concerns?\n\n2. **Experimental Coverage**: The review context lists 20 imagined experiments. Assess which are covered in the paper and which are missing. The paper has 11 main-text tables + 13 appendix tables.\n\n3. **Statistical Rigor**: Bootstrap CIs, TOST equivalence, Granger causality, McNemar tests — are they correctly applied and reported?\n\n4. **Strengths**: List 3–5 specific strengths with evidence.\n\n5. **Weaknesses**: List 3–5 specific weaknesses with evidence. Flag any missing controls, questionable design choices, or gaps.\n\n6. **Questions for Authors**: 3–5 specific questions.\n\n7. **Minor Issues**: Presentation, clarity, missing details.\n\n8. **Score** (1–6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept) with justification.\n\n9. **Confidence**: 1–5 scale.\n\nBe thorough, critical, and fair. Cite specific tables/sections. This is for a top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":42680,"cache_read_input_tokens":24402,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":42680},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4cded27-964c-4711-9220-b05f8309dff9","uuid":"f74ae410-93d2-4065-bc83-5f68a29d6f88"}
