{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019MGSmL2SbX3TijinGMPyDr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{AI-Generated Text Detection}\n5\t\\label{sec:related-detection}\n6\t\n7\tMethods for distinguishing human-written from machine-generated text range from statistical tests \\cite{gltr,grover,release_strategies} through zero-shot approaches \\cite{detectgpt,fast_detectgpt,binoculars,dna_gpt} to learned classifiers \\cite{ghostbuster,feat_aug_transformer,reliable_detection,ld_score}, with extensions to intrinsic dimensionality \\cite{intrinsic_dimension}, surprisal diversity \\cite{diveye}, and distributional gaps \\cite{generator_barrier}; see \\citet{wu_survey_2025} and \\citet{jawahar_survey} for surveys.\n8\tRecent work moves beyond binary decisions---recognizing collaboration roles \\cite{beyond_binary}, joint detection-attribution \\cite{two_birds}, creator-editor modes \\cite{beyond_final_actor}, multi-generator attribution \\cite{semeval2024_task8}---but all target \\emph{source} or \\emph{production mode} taxonomies rather than recursive generation \\emph{depth}; detection scores exhibit high cross-generator variance as depth predictors (binary F1 0.52--0.71; Table~\\ref{tab:baselines}) \\cite{paraphrase_evades,detection_possibilities}.\n9\tOur contribution is the task formulation (generation depth classification \\cite{gutierrez_ordinal_survey,coral_ordinal}) and the finding that combining drifting and stationary features yields consistent cross-generator performance, orthogonal to source attribution \\cite{uchendu_attribution,munir_looking_glass}.\n10\t\n11\t\\subsection{Model Collapse and Iterative Generation}\n12\t\\label{sec:related-collapse}\n13\t\n14\tSelf-consuming generative models progressively lose distributional coverage \\cite{self_consuming_mad,curse_of_recursion}, though data mixing mitigates collapse \\cite{model_collapse_avoidable,strong_model_collapse}; these studies address \\emph{training-loop} degradation, whereas we study \\emph{inference chains} with fixed parameters.\n15\tIterative self-refinement \\cite{self_refine} targets output improvement through feedback rather than unconstrained recursive generation.\n16\tTwo concurrent works are most directly relevant: \\citet{broken_telephone} analyze paraphrasing and translation chains showing progressive information loss, and \\citet{markovian_chains} demonstrate attractor dynamics bounding distinguishable depth to approximately three classes.\n17\tWe differ by studying \\emph{non-paraphrastic continuation} chains, framing depth as \\emph{ordinal classification} (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+), and providing DDR feature-level diagnostics revealing \\emph{what} converges (location) versus what remains stable (shape)---complementing their analysis of \\emph{why} convergence occurs.\n18\tThat all three works independently converge on ${\\sim}$3 distinguishable classes---from Markovian dynamics \\cite{markovian_chains}, information loss \\cite{broken_telephone}, and feature compression (this work)---suggests a shared ceiling.\n19\t\n20\t\\subsection{Vocabulary Diversity and Stylometry}\n21\t\\label{sec:related-diversity}\n22\t\n23\tDeclining lexical diversity in LLM text is documented in training \\cite{curious_decline} and deployment \\cite{writing_diversity}; our surface features (TTR, hapax ratio) are classical stylometric measures \\cite{stamatatos_survey,koppel_authorship} that we extend beyond generator identity \\cite{uchendu_attribution} to the depth dimension under top-$p$ sampling \\cite{nucleus_sampling}.\n24\t\n25\t\\subsection{Data Quality and Provenance}\n26\t\\label{sec:related-provenance}\n27\t\n28\tScalable data curation relies on quality signals \\cite{dsir,qurating,ccnet}; generation depth is a complementary provenance signal.\n29\tProactive watermarking \\cite{watermark_llm,synthid_text,aaronson_watermark} embeds identifiable patterns, but strength decays across multi-step chains \\cite{chainwash}, motivating passive methods; our NLL analysis connects to \\citet{min_k_percent} (probability-tail membership inference), extending to full distributional analysis for depth.\n30\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Aa4LbC8tVN8tPubtpYmsUq","session_id":"d4cded27-964c-4711-9220-b05f8309dff9","uuid":"ac2b5fd8-ad43-42ba-9de8-c80fe5133de5","timestamp":"2026-05-20T10:44:18.883Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YPvJY9KFewwyjCaauMRX9q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EZ7XgNSmgShPRBE4FY3tea","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty, Related Work, and Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Generation Depth Fingerprinting via Differential Depth Response in Non-Paraphrastic LLM Chains\"\n\nThe paper formulates \"generation depth classification\" (gen-0 / gen-1 / gen-2+) as a new task beyond binary AI-text detection. It introduces Differential Depth Response (DDR): when text is recursively generated through non-paraphrastic chains, central-tendency features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) of NLL distributions drift monotonically with depth, while shape features (entropy max, tail decay) remain near-stationary.\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. Task formulation: generation depth classification (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) in non-paraphrastic continuation chains\n2. Empirical characterization of DDR across 8 generators, 5 families, 3 prompt types, 4 domains + causality analysis\n3. Negative finding: depth-aware data curation ≈ random subsampling for downstream CLM quality\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)**:\n- Perplexity-based detection: DetectGPT, FastDetectGPT, Binoculars, GLTR, DNA-GPT — all binary classifiers\n- Distributional signatures/LD-Score: character-level detection, used as baseline and augmentation feature\n- Model collapse: Shumailov et al. (2024), Dohmatob et al. (2024) — aggregate distributional consequences, not individual text depth\n- Iterative generation chains: Briesch et al. (2025) \"LLM as Broken Telephone\" — paraphrastic chains with attractor convergence\n- Vocabulary diversity/stylometry: TTR, hapax — repurposed as depth-discriminative features\n- Data quality/curation: Wenzek et al. (2019) CCNet\n\n**Method**: 7 handcrafted features from NLL distribution under Pythia-410M reference model → XGBoost + LOGO evaluation. Binary F1 = 0.694 (LOGO), 0.830 (cross-model chains).\n\n**Key experimental results**:\n- LOGO 5-gen average: macro F1 = 0.804, binary F1 = 0.694\n- Baseline comparison: 7-feat (0.798) > Avg-LogProb (0.765) > LD-Score (0.721) > FastDetectGPT (0.627) > Binoculars (0.579)\n- Cross-model chains: binary F1 = 0.830\n- Cross-reference validation: 3 models all preserve DDR\n- DDR causality: 6/7 Granger-significant, 5.3× permutation gap\n- Downstream: negative finding (TOST equivalence)\n- Instruct models: substantial degradation (base-only scope)\n- 5-class ceiling at ~0.50\n- QLoRA end-to-end: +10–15 pp over 7-feat but 15× cost\n- Temperature: degrades at t > 0.9\n- Length confound controlled\n- Paraphrastic chains: much weaker signal (0.62 vs 0.781)\n\n**Paper structure**: 8 pages main text + 6 pages appendix (A–L). 11 references.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: \n   - Is the task formulation genuinely new? How does it differ from existing work (especially Briesch et al. 2025)?\n   - Is DDR a new concept or a repackaging of known vocabulary impoverishment?\n   - Does the paper adequately distinguish itself from model collapse literature?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**:\n   - Are there missing related works? (Consider: watermarking, membership inference, data provenance tracking, synthetic data detection beyond the cited methods, iterative self-improvement/self-play literature)\n   - Is the positioning against Briesch et al. (2025) convincing?\n\n3. **Contribution Significance**:\n   - Is the contribution sufficient for a top venue? \n   - The 7-feature ensemble only beats Avg-LogProb by 3.3 pp — is this enough?\n   - How impactful is the negative finding (Claim 3)?\n\n4. **Scope and Limitations**:\n   - Base models only — how limiting is this for practical relevance?\n   - 3-class ceiling — does this limit the task's utility?\n   - Temperature sensitivity — realistic deployment concern?\n\n5. **Strengths**: List 3–5 specific strengths.\n\n6. **Weaknesses**: List 3–5 specific weaknesses.\n\n7. **Questions for Authors**: 3–5 questions.\n\n8. **Minor Issues**: Writing quality, missing citations, presentation.\n\n9. **Score** (1–6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept) with justification.\n\n10. **Confidence**: 1–5 scale.\n\nBe thorough, critical, and fair. This is for a top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":42680,"cache_read_input_tokens":24402,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":42680},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4cded27-964c-4711-9220-b05f8309dff9","uuid":"2c1a225a-01cc-4700-aa24-8647b5b0670b"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a9f2f15ea1f135d59","tool_use_id":"toolu_01EZ7XgNSmgShPRBE4FY3tea","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty, Related Work, and Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Generation Depth Fingerprinting via Differential Depth Response in Non-Paraphrastic LLM Chains\"\n\nThe paper formulates \"generation depth classification\" (gen-0 / gen-1 / gen-2+) as a new task beyond binary AI-text detection. It introduces Differential Depth Response (DDR): when text is recursively generated through non-paraphrastic chains, central-tendency features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) of NLL distributions drift monotonically with depth, while shape features (entropy max, tail decay) remain near-stationary.\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. Task formulation: generation depth classification (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) in non-paraphrastic continuation chains\n2. Empirical characterization of DDR across 8 generators, 5 families, 3 prompt types, 4 domains + causality analysis\n3. Negative finding: depth-aware data curation ≈ random subsampling for downstream CLM quality\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)**:\n- Perplexity-based detection: DetectGPT, FastDetectGPT, Binoculars, GLTR, DNA-GPT — all binary classifiers\n- Distributional signatures/LD-Score: character-level detection, used as baseline and augmentation feature\n- Model collapse: Shumailov et al. (2024), Dohmatob et al. (2024) — aggregate distributional consequences, not individual text depth\n- Iterative generation chains: Briesch et al. (2025) \"LLM as Broken Telephone\" — paraphrastic chains with attractor convergence\n- Vocabulary diversity/stylometry: TTR, hapax — repurposed as depth-discriminative features\n- Data quality/curation: Wenzek et al. (2019) CCNet\n\n**Method**: 7 handcrafted features from NLL distribution under Pythia-410M reference model → XGBoost + LOGO evaluation. Binary F1 = 0.694 (LOGO), 0.830 (cross-model chains).\n\n**Key experimental results**:\n- LOGO 5-gen average: macro F1 = 0.804, binary F1 = 0.694\n- Baseline comparison: 7-feat (0.798) > Avg-LogProb (0.765) > LD-Score (0.721) > FastDetectGPT (0.627) > Binoculars (0.579)\n- Cross-model chains: binary F1 = 0.830\n- Cross-reference validation: 3 models all preserve DDR\n- DDR causality: 6/7 Granger-significant, 5.3× permutation gap\n- Downstream: negative finding (TOST equivalence)\n- Instruct models: substantial degradation (base-only scope)\n- 5-class ceiling at ~0.50\n- QLoRA end-to-end: +10–15 pp over 7-feat but 15× cost\n- Temperature: degrades at t > 0.9\n- Length confound controlled\n- Paraphrastic chains: much weaker signal (0.62 vs 0.781)\n\n**Paper structure**: 8 pages main text + 6 pages appendix (A–L). 11 references.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: \n   - Is the task formulation genuinely new? How does it differ from existing work (especially Briesch et al. 2025)?\n   - Is DDR a new concept or a repackaging of known vocabulary impoverishment?\n   - Does the paper adequately distinguish itself from model collapse literature?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**:\n   - Are there missing related works? (Consider: watermarking, membership inference, data provenance tracking, synthetic data detection beyond the cited methods, iterative self-improvement/self-play literature)\n   - Is the positioning against Briesch et al. (2025) convincing?\n\n3. **Contribution Significance**:\n   - Is the contribution sufficient for a top venue? \n   - The 7-feature ensemble only beats Avg-LogProb by 3.3 pp — is this enough?\n   - How impactful is the negative finding (Claim 3)?\n\n4. **Scope and Limitations**:\n   - Base models only — how limiting is this for practical relevance?\n   - 3-class ceiling — does this limit the task's utility?\n   - Temperature sensitivity — realistic deployment concern?\n\n5. **Strengths**: List 3–5 specific strengths.\n\n6. **Weaknesses**: List 3–5 specific weaknesses.\n\n7. **Questions for Authors**: 3–5 questions.\n\n8. **Minor Issues**: Writing quality, missing citations, presentation.\n\n9. **Score** (1–6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept) with justification.\n\n10. **Confidence**: 1–5 scale.\n\nBe thorough, critical, and fair. This is for a top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate.","uuid":"e4591be4-d72e-4614-a14b-97d19142c057","session_id":"d4cded27-964c-4711-9220-b05f8309dff9"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty, Related Work, and Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Generation Depth Fingerprinting via Differential Depth Response in Non-Paraphrastic LLM Chains\"\n\nThe paper formulates \"generation depth classification\" (gen-0 / gen-1 / gen-2+) as a new task beyond binary AI-text detection. It introduces Differential Depth Response (DDR): when text is recursively generated through non-paraphrastic chains, central-tendency features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) of NLL distributions drift monotonically with depth, while shape features (entropy max, tail decay) remain near-stationary.\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. Task formulation: generation depth classification (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) in non-paraphrastic continuation chains\n2. Empirical characterization of DDR across 8 generators, 5 families, 3 prompt types, 4 domains + causality analysis\n3. Negative finding: depth-aware data curation ≈ random subsampling for downstream CLM quality\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)**:\n- Perplexity-based detection: DetectGPT, FastDetectGPT, Binoculars, GLTR, DNA-GPT — all binary classifiers\n- Distributional signatures/LD-Score: character-level detection, used as baseline and augmentation feature\n- Model collapse: Shumailov et al. (2024), Dohmatob et al. (2024) — aggregate distributional consequences, not individual text depth\n- Iterative generation chains: Briesch et al. (2025) \"LLM as Broken Telephone\" — paraphrastic chains with attractor convergence\n- Vocabulary diversity/stylometry: TTR, hapax — repurposed as depth-discriminative features\n- Data quality/curation: Wenzek et al. (2019) CCNet\n\n**Method**: 7 handcrafted features from NLL distribution under Pythia-410M reference model → XGBoost + LOGO evaluation. Binary F1 = 0.694 (LOGO), 0.830 (cross-model chains).\n\n**Key experimental results**:\n- LOGO 5-gen average: macro F1 = 0.804, binary F1 = 0.694\n- Baseline comparison: 7-feat (0.798) > Avg-LogProb (0.765) > LD-Score (0.721) > FastDetectGPT (0.627) > Binoculars (0.579)\n- Cross-model chains: binary F1 = 0.830\n- Cross-reference validation: 3 models all preserve DDR\n- DDR causality: 6/7 Granger-significant, 5.3× permutation gap\n- Downstream: negative finding (TOST equivalence)\n- Instruct models: substantial degradation (base-only scope)\n- 5-class ceiling at ~0.50\n- QLoRA end-to-end: +10–15 pp over 7-feat but 15× cost\n- Temperature: degrades at t > 0.9\n- Length confound controlled\n- Paraphrastic chains: much weaker signal (0.62 vs 0.781)\n\n**Paper structure**: 8 pages main text + 6 pages appendix (A–L). 11 references.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: \n   - Is the task formulation genuinely new? How does it differ from existing work (especially Briesch et al. 2025)?\n   - Is DDR a new concept or a repackaging of known vocabulary impoverishment?\n   - Does the paper adequately distinguish itself from model collapse literature?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**:\n   - Are there missing related works? (Consider: watermarking, membership inference, data provenance tracking, synthetic data detection beyond the cited methods, iterative self-improvement/self-play literature)\n   - Is the positioning against Briesch et al. (2025) convincing?\n\n3. **Contribution Significance**:\n   - Is the contribution sufficient for a top venue? \n   - The 7-feature ensemble only beats Avg-LogProb by 3.3 pp — is this enough?\n   - How impactful is the negative finding (Claim 3)?\n\n4. **Scope and Limitations**:\n   - Base models only — how limiting is this for practical relevance?\n   - 3-class ceiling — does this limit the task's utility?\n   - Temperature sensitivity — realistic deployment concern?\n\n5. **Strengths**: List 3–5 specific strengths.\n\n6. **Weaknesses**: List 3–5 specific weaknesses.\n\n7. **Questions for Authors**: 3–5 questions.\n\n8. **Minor Issues**: Writing quality, missing citations, presentation.\n\n9. **Score** (1–6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept) with justification.\n\n10. **Confidence**: 1–5 scale.\n\nBe thorough, critical, and fair. This is for a top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EZ7XgNSmgShPRBE4FY3tea","session_id":"d4cded27-964c-4711-9220-b05f8309dff9","uuid":"24a5805f-d761-4c95-a3ba-49929249f81c","timestamp":"2026-05-20T10:44:21.443Z"}
